Alpha engineers, nor do they concur with its content:  Alpha was
chugging right along the path laid out for it years ago, with no
unexpected recent hiccups - until Compaq pulled out the rug on June
25th), and other Alpha architects (plus one high-level Alpha
development manager) have privately agreed with him.  Not one has come
forward to support Compaq's version.

Inconsistently, Compaq also, via employee newsgroup posts and via a
discussion Mark Gorham and VMS engineers had with 'Alphaman' (see
c.o.v. posts the week of the announcement), stated that Alpha
technology would be used to 'rescue' the failing IA64 architecture,
resulting in technology equivalent to what Alpha otherwise would have
been - and in a time-frame that would benefit the VMS port by the time
it was completed.  This lie was so incompetent (and likely so
unwelcome when Intel heard about it) that it died out very quickly -
but it nonetheless happened, and Compaq is accountable for it.

And Compaq presented Alpha development costs as being unsupportable
(see a June 27th eWeek interview with Winkler, plus additional drivel
