Brannon Batson said so here, or in comp.arch (or both).

> [...] from day one I heard it as Compaq would have trouble maintaining its
> performance edge over IPF three generations out.  

So Compaq stops competing now, because their performance edge _may_ be
eroding three generations out - i.e., at least five years from now?

> My understanding of what drove the decision was more than pure chip speed.

That's not what was said or implied in publich statements, and in fact from a
technical point of view, is even more unlikely. See below.

> I was told upfront that it was at the system performance level that became
> the issue.  Remember Alpha's performance advantage was both its chip speed
> and the fact that it was 64 bit.  What the team saw who was building the
> follow-on to the Wildfire was the IPF systems would close in on Alpha's
> systems in the future - it was never ever expressed to me as Alpha systems
> would have trouble "keeping up".

From a systems' point of view this is complete nonsense. The EV7, then
recently completed, has features that make building large-scale systems
much easier than with any other processor on the market or the roadmaps.
Thus, building large IPF systems will take much more effort in designing
and building the systems interconnect than building large Alpha (EV7 and
follow-on) systems. There is nothing disclosed about IPF or any future
implementation of the IA64 architecture that makes it even remotely plausible
this claim could be true.

>     "This is a very bold move for us," said Rich Marcello,
