Erkki Aalto <aalto@pcu.helsinki.fi.invalid> wrote in message news:<9mnjo8$sdd$1@oravannahka.helsinki.fi>...
> Mauce <mauce@cyberdude.com> wrote:
> : Hi,
>  
> : When a user logs in the following messages appears:
> : "Account is disabled -- see Account Administrator"
> : I'd seen this before, at that time I could unlock the account by entering:
> : "usermod -x administrative_lock_applies=0 <uid>"

> 1) Too many unsuccesfull login attempts. 
> The count is in the field u_numunsuclog
> 
> 2) The user's password is over a year old.
> In that case remove the entire u_succhg field. Then the user can
> log in but is requested to change password immediately.
> 
> 3) The u_lock@ field is missing

Here's a few other things to check:

if u_succhg (last time the password was successfully changed) is
older than u_exp you'll only be forced to change the password on
login, but if it is older than u_life, the account will be disabled.
This can be worked around by having root set the password.

if u_numunsuclog is greater than u_maxtries, the account will be
disabled.

If those don't fix the problem, look at u_max_login_intvl; exceeding
this can also disable the account.

All of these values are in seconds; either as an interval or as a
timestamp (number of seconds since the epoch, 00:00 1 Jan 1970 UTC)

HTH

