Interesting that you chose today, and CA as an example, to take a swipe at
the free market economy.

Today, August 29, 2001, is the stockholders meeting for Computer Assosciates.

A huge proxy battle has been underway for a few months.  A faction of
disgruntled stockholders is attempting to rid the company of several of
the "honchos".  The issues seem to be:

Overpayed honchos
Poor treatment of the customers
Poor treatment of employees
Weakening overall performance of the company

This is not a bunch of stockholders peeved because of bad results for a
quarter or two.

With any luck, 4 CA executives will be looking for new jobs pretty soon.


It seems to me, the problem is not the free market economy, but rather
shareholders who pay too little attention.  If shareholders expect the BOD
to protect their interests, but few of them ever express their
displeasure, a company can gradually rot away.  If the shareholders are
mainly well-informed and attentive, the BOD is more likely to behave well.

I wonder if the recent popularity of "day trading" and similar strategies,
as opposed to buy-and-hold investing, is contributing to crappy corporate
management.  Many of the new sorts of investors know little or nothing
about the firm's business, and don't give a hoot about its long-term
health.

