[ Jeff Killeen ]

>>  Are you suggesting AIX or Linux is as robust and as mature as
>>  OpenVMS when it comes to being a solid enterprise class server?

Well, part of the answer clearly depends on how one defines "robust" and
"mature".  I have essentialy zero experience with AIX, and I think it's
fair to say that Linux still has some distance to go.  (Hey, I run Linux
on my laptop, and in what seems to be an attempt to provide people with
something approximating the look'n'feel of Windows, not only does Linux
come with window managers which try to be vaguely Windows-like, but the
OS takes the attempt at Microsoft emulation one step further, by hanging
and freezing every now and then for no apparent reason. ;-)

However, as for other versions of UNIX, here's some "uptime" output from
a couple of our servers:

 4:23pm  up 764 days,  1:03,  22 users,  load average: 9.12, 9.08, 9.00
 4:23pm  up 322 days, 18:42,  3 users,  load average: 0.81, 0.78, 0.73

The first is from a 12-processor Origin 2000; actually, IRIX's "uptime"
has a bug, and it hasn't *really* been up for more than two years.  But
it is true that its only downtime has been for OS upgrades and scheduled
power outages, going back at least a year.

The second is from a 64-processor HP V2500 system, and that uptime is in
fact real.  (By the way, HP-UX normalizes the load average to the number
of CPUs, so that represents about 52 of 64 processors busy doing work.)

Both systems have been cranking away on a wide variety of jobs, for all
the time that they've been running.  So I'd certainly put those in the
"robust" category.  (And, for high-performance technical computing, in
the "mature" category as well.  Probably more mature than OpenVMS, for
that matter.)

