On 29 Aug 2001 17:14:01 +0200, Jan C Vorbrueggen
<vorbrjz6@sunu513.rz.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> wrote:

>Alan Greig <a.greig@virgin.net> writes:
>
>> Having been recently playing with TOPS-20 again under the simh
>> emulator it;'s amazing just how well even TOPS-20 4.1 (around 1978)
>> stands up against VMS 7.3 from a user interface point of view.
>
>Honest question: having used TOPS-10, how different is it in this respect,
>and where do you see VMS's deficiencies compared to TOPS-20?

This could get a bit religious but... TOPS-10 was a descendent of the
original DEC timesharing O/S for the PDP-6. As such it predates a lot
of the features usually associated with something like Unix or VMS
(limited virtual memory support, simple file names and directory
structure, limited command interpreter, single process per job etc).
But it did have excellent timesharing capabilities, layered products,
cusps and user written software.  

TOPS-20 grew out of BBN Tenex which was written for the first PDP-10
(KA-10) under a contract to Arpa to write a mainframe Arpanet
operating system. BBN being Bolt Beranek and Newman of Arpanet fame.
In many ways TOPS-20 was a mainframe superset of Unix with
Arpanet/Internet support built in from 1969!!   The operating system
was later commercialized by DEC as TOPS-20. A typical TOPS-20 system
would require more memory than TOPS-10 to handle the same workload but
provided all the bells and whistles of a modern command interface
(command completion/recognition, powerful command interpretor with
scriptable extensions (PCL, MIC, .CMD files etc) available. Much of
what you might typically do with .COM files in DCL and utilities was
supported directly by the EXEC command processor. For example
TDIRECTORY would give you a directory sorted by date order,

TOPS-20 also supported TOPS-10 code transparent to the user via the
PA1050 emulator. So you could compile/link/run code containing TOPS-10
system calls and even run TOPS-10 executables directly. Obviously you
could also compile native code!

One example where I still thinks TOPS-20 beats VMS is in its command
processor. Being able to give a command to produce a list of files
sorted by date they were last read for instance. Assuring PCL
extensions was installed then you could use an Algol like language to
add your own commands. These could then be compiled for speed of
execution. Filenames by default were upper case but you could prefix a
character with CTRL-V to quote it and allow lower case or any other
character in a filename.  Another point was that most of what VMS
provides as distinct executables (DIR, AUTHORIZE, etc) was built
directly into the command processor reducing image activation.

The Galaxy BATCH/PRINT system and OPR Operator interface was as least
as powerful as that possessed by VMS today even in 1980.

I could go on but that will do for now.  


--
Alan


