In article <KiTh7.295$Ia1.66096@typhoon1.gnilink.net>,
Jeff Killeen <Jeff@IDM-IO.com> wrote:
> 
> I am firm believer that Compaq's (Digital's) most painful wounds are self
> inflicted.

:-)  That might be common ground for you and Mr. Todd.

>  I do not claim to be expert on other platforms an but haven't
> both IBM and SUN put their customers through major hardware and software
> changes that forced source level recompiles?

Sun's move from Motorola 68xxx to sparc in the 80s caused the need for
customer recompiles, but that's the only case I'm aware of from IBM or Sun.
I've seen some scary old code from AIX 3.2.x that will still run on AIX 4.3.x,
and (like Sun) ILP32 binaries will run on a version of the OS that's been
booted with an LP64 kernel.  I don't follow IBM's mainframes or AS/400 systems
(or whatever they're calling them these days), so I'm not up on history for
them.

>  If so technically how are they
> any less reliable as a business partner?  I think the answer is technically
> they aren't any less reliable but the did it with a lot more finesse and
> didn't self-inflict wounds...

You're right that it's the manner in which it was done that's caused the
most concern.  I think when Sun moved from 68xxx to sparc, everyone knew
in advance the move was coming, the performance benefits at the time
were obvious, and Sun made sure that customers knew that 68xxx binaries
would be available during the transition period.  Of these three things,
only the last has really been done by Compaq.

Also, especially in Sun's case, they were (and still are) a "one trick pony".
There was no real concern in anyone's mind that Sun was going to quit doing
UNIX and leave customers with a "dead end" after they spent years transitioning
to a new architecture.  Of course, it turns out that Sun *did* do something
like that a few years later (the move from BSD SunOS 4.x to Solaris 2.x),
but even then it wasn't the same kind of "dead end" that VMS or Tru64 UNIX
customers are thinking may be coming in a couple years.  You could still buy
SunOS 4.x for *years* after Solaris was Sun's bread-and-butter.

Architecture switches have to happen once in a great while, but they're
usually only done by a company that you know has a committment to the products
making the switch, and they're done when the need for the arch switch becomes
overwhelmingly obvious to all.  The concerns many Compaq customers have is
whether that committment truly is there, on the part of Compaq.

As we've seen for the past two months, people are also willing to debate
whether the need for an arch switch is certain.  Only the brightest Alpha
engineers probably really know for certain, and we'll probably never get
to hear their candid answer to that question is.

The question itself is moot, at this point.  The move is going to happen.
The real question is "What enterprise products will still be alive three
or five years after the architecture transition happens?".  I think that's
what has a lot of potential Compaq customers concerned.

Tim
