And the outcome of this meeting is yes we will stop buying Alpha
workstations for Finite Element Analysis Also the plan to migrate a
SAP/NT installation to Tru64 is almost totally gone now and even a US
VAX to Alpha migration now seems unlikely (probably upgrade the
VAXcluster for peanuts on the used market). Despite my anger here I
have continued to argue that we should not totally rule out further
Alpha purchases but I am now in a minority of one it seems.

We will continue to run the current XP1000s under NT but will evaluate
an IA64 box with Ansys (likely from anyone *but* Compaq) and a 2GHz
Pentium 4 (or similar) asap.

Compaq senior management no longer answer my emails (and they are
polite), Compaq sales seem to have gone into hiding on over the
SAP/Tru64 proposal and none of my outstanding questions about the VMS
port have yet been answered. 

Compaq policy seems to be if you don't like what the customers say
then stick your fingers in your ears and watch the whole house of
cards collapse around you.

Just two years ago we seriously considered buying 30 XP1000s for
Unigraphics (this location only - over 200 hundred worldwide)  but
were saved by EDS dropping support for both Alpha/NT and Tru64 because
of lack of demand and lack of confidence in Compaq's commitment to
Alpha. How right they were. So we stuck with HP for Unigraphics and
spent around half a million dollars with them instead.

When I started with my current employer three years ago we had zero
Alpha systems on site. Currently at this one location we have nine.
Not enough for Compaq to care about obviously and I don't know why I
bothered. Compaq definitely have the DEC disease and I'm afraid it's
fatal.

I really would hate to be working inside Compaq and read stuff like
this right now but believe me folks I hate writing it. And just a few
months ago after the well attended London VMS technical update event I
finally convinced myself Compaq were serious and that things were
brighter than they had been for many years. Foolish. Please guys, if
you read this, find some way to get this port out of the door before
the curtain comes down.


--
Alan


