Given the attitude of the Encompass leadership to date, I suspect they'll
have plenty of sympathetic supporters there.  And given the attitude of at
least a significant portion of the customer base, failure to step up to the
plate for such a session could have been pretty expensive.

Wish I could be there, so I'll look forward to hearing how it turns out.  I
somewhat pity Rich, since I suspect he's going to have to stand up there
without anything more substantial than what we've already heard.  But he can
perhaps deflect the awkward issues of the lies about Alpha's performance
potential and cost-effectiveness by pretending that they were 'judgement
calls' rather than outright fabrications, and do the same in support of the
'value' of the IA64 platform's 'industry-standardness' (without confronting
the issue of its current non-existence).  It'll still be kind of difficult
to justify the impact on VMS and Tru64 sales during the period when these
systems are available only on a lame-duck hardware platform, but since the
Q3 figures won't yet be available he may be able to skate around this one as
well.

(I'm not being snide at Rich here:  he's got responsibilities to get Compaq
through a tight spot here and will simply try to acquit himself as best he
can - likely without deriving much enjoyment from the process.)

Rich is a personable guy and likely has the advantage of not being
personally responsible for the most dubious aspects of this debacle, so he
may well escape relatively unscathed.  The real test will be how well the
actual content of the session stands up to later scrutiny after the good
feelings are factored out (very much as in the discussion of Don's post
earlier today).

