This'll be an interesting one too.  Of course, Compaq would seem to have
absolutely zero say in how Intel chooses to integrate Alpha technology (or
not), so one would hope to see a liberal sprinkling of Intel architects on
the panel speaking officially for Intel.

I'm still betting that the gist will be that at most only minor tweaks will
get to market in IA64 before 2005 (i.e., nothing major by the time the VMS
port is scheduled to ship), then the peripheral EV7 stuff (on-chip glue for
RAMBUS and SMP) in 2005, and maybe some OOO and/or SMT in 2006 or 2007.
Aside from details about future IA64 features (information on which has been
conspicuously sparse by comparison with Alpha's road maps), dates are the
important thing:  people can promise *anything* if they don't have to say
when it will appear, and the on-time delivery history of IA64 makes any
schedule that appears at all aggressive subject to serious question.

