Lets assume you are correct that the _chip_ is slower.  If it turns out that
Compaq can deliver the _system_ with equal to or greater performance for
equal to or less costs who cares?  Isn't total system performance at cost
that matters?  If they can deliver the same performance with a 2-way or
4-way IPF for the same cost as a single chip Alpha who cares.

Our segment of the industry has a long history of "cheaper" being bundled up
to take on "faster", at the same price point, going all the way back to the
VAX 11/780 cluster.  With InfiniBand and Blade servers (effectively clusters
in a small rack) the world is changing.

Just so everyone knows my feelings on the subject long pre-date the June
25th announcement.  I used to go to User Group meetings with Compaq and hear
the great and wonderful things they were going to do to create market
identity for Alpha.  I was often the lone voice saying pushing Alpha won't
work.  Companies buy solutions and not chips.  They could care less if it
runs on the foobar chip or whatever.  If they were going to lead with
technology they should have lead with OpenVMS.  By themselves Alpha chips
only provide a solution to heating a small room.  Understand in many ways I
am chuckling today at the mess that Compaq now finds itself in by having
created its image based on the chip rather than the systems.

In the end I want to know that Compaq will deliver systems with the same or
better performance for the same of less cost.  I don't care if they do it
with 1, 2, or 4 chips...





