Jeff-

You made some interesting points, and although I'm a fan of the Alpha,
overall I agree with your statement that it's the total solution that matters.

The problem is that Compaq has now changed directions enough times in
the last few years that people are really questioning whether *any* current
enterprise product (or solution) from Compaq has long-term viability.  Why
deploy a solution that involves OpenVMS or Tru64 on any chip, when the
company delivering the solution may end the long-term viability of that
solution in a couple years?

We both know that superior technology is rarely the deciding factor (i.e.
inferior technology often outlasts and eventually kills off superior
technology for various reasons), so when companies like IBM and Sun offer
a competing solution, why would any enterprise IT Manager choose Compaq's
solution, these days?  You can say what you want about IBM and Sun (I'm
no fan of Sun, the SPARC, or Solaris) or IBM (I'm ok with them), but IT
Managers can at least really believe that neither of those two companies
is going to abruptly change course or suddenly ditch a critical component
of the solutions they provide to customers.

I've been a fan of Digital products for a few years, and I liked the direction
I saw Tru64 UNIX going in the last year -- additional market penetration,
which improves app availability.  It was hard for me to write the previous
two paragraphs, but even fans of Digital's and now Compaq's product offerings
have to be asking themselves, "What's next?".

As a side note, one of the trade rags that I read recently rumored that
a precipitous drop in sales of Alpha systems after the omega announcement
was likely the final nail in the coffin for Midwest Systems, which closed
its doors recently. The current economy certainly was the major factor, but
the uncertainty with Compaq is a big one too.  Customers, even customers
that look, are having a hard time finding a reason to buy enterprise
solutions from Compaq, when the solution-provider's committment to those
solutions is questionable.

Tim
