It is hard to imagine any firmer public statements of commitment than Compaq
made about its long-term commitments to Alpha.  If you don't therefore
understand that Compaq's public commitments are utterly worthless, you're
completely divorced from reality.

...

> I do get frustrated when I see the discussion focusing on the esoteric
> aspects of the chips - one way or another those will be solved - either
> though elegant engineering or brute force (n-way processor systems).  The
> real issues IMO are the non-technical issues that relate to doing business
> with Compaq.

Exactly.  But you don't seem to understand that these 'esoteric aspects' are
very relevant to such issues of doing business with Compaq - in particular,
issues of trust.

That's because Compaq itself made these 'fine points' issues when it lied
about Alpha's performance potential compared with IA64's.  It's these *lies*
that are important to most people perhaps more than even a factor of 2 in
per-processor performance (though that's not exactly chopped liver,
either) - but to establish that they were in fact lies, one must examine the
details of the chip architectures to see why Compaq's assertions were wholly
unbelievable.

But while on the subject of performance, in later posts you've asserted that
performance isn't an issue because Compaq will be able to substitute 2 or 3
IA64 processors for each Alpha chip.  That seems a fairly naive suggestion,
since the difference in cost between building a uniprocessor and a
dual-processor system, or between a dual-processor and a 4-processor system,
is usually considerably greater than the mere cost of the additional
processors (i.e., SMP scaling increases the expense of the surrounding
hardware as well - especially when the IA64 chips don't have as much
built-in SMP glue as, say, EV7 does).

You've said that a $500 - $1000 difference (should extra IA64 processors be
required to compensate for a loss in performance) in the price of a server
system isn't important, and I agree.  But this difference is at least as
much as the per-processor development-cost overhead that Compaq complained
was so burdensome that it couldn't afford to continue developing Alpha (and
part of the argument I've used to assert that this was yet another lie).

