This is growing somewhat off-topic, but there's a very big difference
between a low-end dual processor system and a good dual processor
system.  A lot of people got excited about the Abit BP6 motherboard,
because you could put two celerons in it, which Intel didn't want
anybody to do.  So, lots of people were able to have dual processor
machines and feel really important for it.  But I've worked with such
machines, as well as better-engineered dual processor workstation class
machines, and I can ensure you that the Abit was a pretty lousy dual
processor.  The memory controller could not feed both CPU's demand
simultaneously, which meant that if you ran two STREAM benchmarks at
the same time, you wouldn't get 2X scaling.  On dual processor
workstations with better memory throughput, you get 2X scaling.
STREAM is just a benchmark, but for some of my jobs I am RAM bound as the
CPUs are pulling in 2 big floating point matrices and performing fairly
simple operations like Z=X+Y.  In that case, I found that having 2 cpus
did not allow me to run 2 jobs at the same time and achieve 2X throughput.
It was more like 1.6 throughput, but then again, it only cost me 1.6
as much to get the dual processor machine.

Where it gets really interesting is 4 cpus or higher on the same
board.  I gained some enlightenment when I ran stream on our ES40 (4
667MHz cpus, I think), and it scaled linearly up to 4 cpus.  I guess
that crossbar switch to the memory where each switch port is really fat
makes a difference.

Dave


