I just looked at the man page for setpgid() and setpgrp() and it does
look confusing.  It would appear that the number and type of arguments
is a function of which compiler options are specified.

You may want to read the "man 5 standards" man page and see which
compiler options are needed to use setpgrp(void) syntax.  I do not
think setpgid(void) is ever valid, only setpgrp(void) if and only if
the right compiler switches are specified.

Of course the question is, why do you even care to use the
setpgrp(void) notation.  It would seem that setpgid(0,0) should do the
same things as setpgrp(void).

But then again, I do not play in this space, so I am just going by what
I'm reading in the man pages.  Since I have not first hand experience
using either of these functions, I'll defer to someone that knows more
about them.

