Next you can either choose...

    HPS Strategy (AKA Tru64/OpenVMS) with Howard Elias
    or
    Access Strategy (AKA everything below the serves) with John Stautner

Then after we break for lunch Encompass requested the following session (we
wrote the abstract)...

    1673 You Can Bet Your Business on Compaq and Itanium

    Abstract: This session addresses fundamental business issues related to
    Compaq's AlphaT to Itanium strategy. These issues include: Preserving
your
    already existing investment in your Tru64T UNIX or OpenVMST IT
    infrastructure; Why the investment you make today in new projects
deployed
    on Tru64 UNIX  or OpenVMS, both in terms of hardware and application
    development costs, will be preserved in the future; What Compaq is doing
to
     insure the long term  viability of OpenVMS and Tru64 UNIX in the
marketplace
    as technology leading enterprise class servers. These issues and others
are
    covered, and this session provides the answers to why Compaq's Alpha to
Itanium
    strategy is a safe bet for your business.

...we requested session because as we learned more about the technical
aspects of porting from Alpha to VMS many of us realized that it was NOT
going to be mission impossible.  Anyone with open mind who has been reading
'Hoff' Hoffman's postings in comp.os.vms knows that there has already been a
lot of thought put into this and this not some wild ass dream of the
beancounters Houston - there is significant engineering insight behind this.
However, even if you are a believer that the technical issues can be
addressed we kept hearing back over and over again that folks were not
believers about the all the business issue aspects.  We asked for this
session up front and we were very clear that a ra ra "gloss over the issues
with a thin coating of happy dust" would NOT work.  Frankly I thought Compaq
was going to duck the session, especially given the expectation that a
superficial session wouldn't work, and we were pleasantly surprised that
they took the session right away even after we explained our perception of
the situation.  You have to give them credit for being willing to lead to
what may be the hardest session.

