>How fast does the emulator run versus the actual hardware?

I haven't done any real benchmarks but on a 500 Mhz Pentium III things
look at least as fast as a real 2020 (the baby of the PDP-10 family)
and a 2020 was around 1/5th speed of a KL10 from distant memory (I
last used a 2020 in 1985). So the 2020 was comparable to a VAX 11/780
(or maybe 750). So my guess is that on most modern hardware the
emulation is somewhere between the speed of a real KS10 and a KL10. On
an 833Mhz EV68 it could well be faster than any real PDP-10 ever was
but not as fast as Jupiter (the canceled 'PDP20') was supposed to be.

I've just typed in  the following code:
 $TYPE LOOP.MAC
         TITLE LOOP
         SEARCH MONSYM,MACSYM
         .REQUIRE SYS:MACREL

 START:  MOVE 1,COUNT
         SOJL 1,ELOOP
         JRST .-1
 ELOOP:  TMSG <FINISHED
 >
         HALTF
 COUNT:  ^D10000000
         END START

This decrements a memory location from 10 million and took 6 seconds
to execute on a PIII 500 Mhz under Windows 2000. Two instructions are
executed in the loop (SOJL - Subtract One Jump if Less than zero; JRST
- Jump and ReSTore) so that;s 20 million instructions in 6 seconds or
better than 3 million PDP-10 instructions per second. I think that's
close to a KL10 for this particular test.
--
Alan


