The original false "hip pocket" posting read like something from a 11 year
old who was using daddy's computer and should be treated as such.

About the comment below.  The more one knows about the decision the more one
comes to see the logic of it was unavoidable.  I also believe the decision
likely saved VMS rather than killed it.  IMO economics would have required
killing it within 5 years if it stayed on the Alpha chip.

Talking recently with some Compaq folks they pointed out to me that Alpha
had two advantages - clock speed and it was 64-bit.  People tend to focus on
the clock speed but it was also the 64-bit architecture that gave Alpha its
performance advantage.   When one goes beyond clock speed one begins to
understand that once well developed high volume 64-bit processors become
available a major Alpha advantage goes away - even if Alpha could maintain
better clock speed.

The other issue they pointed out was with blade servers and InfiniBand the
mainstream dynamics of computing power change.  When you have a rack of 30+
processors plus storage all attached to Infiniband does a CPU that is twice
the speed, at 2.5X the cost over another 64-bit processor, really buy you
much.  In almost all cases the answer is no.  In that environment CPU speed
isn't the factor it use to be.  That is the environment we are heading
towards and even the Windows folks are trying to build for it...

    http://www.compaq.com/manage/rpm.html

The more information someone gets the more they understand this was the
inevitable conclusion that one comes to when the analysis is done without
emotion.  Bob the reason why Terry has been saying what he has been saying
since 6/25 isn't because he is towing the company line but because he has
been ahead of us on the information curve.  Once everyone who can look at
this issue without the emotion gets all the data they will see nothing
nefarious about Terry's analysis.

The only thing that stands between Compaq and success is can they pull they
conversion off in 24 months.  If they do few will argue that it wasn't a
really smart move.  It they don't pull it off in 36 months IMO they did
nothing to speed up the end of life of VMS anyway...


