"Ginny E. Kim" wrote:

> > Well, you are using the M_NOWAIT flag, which tells malloc that if it
> > can't fulfill your request *immediately* then it must return NULL.
> > Can you change it to use M_WAITOK?
>
> Thank you for your advice.
> I figured out using M_WAITOK. what's difference of two within kernel?
> M_NOWAIT option has no problem.
> Can you explain their meaning more detaily, or recommend related documents?

I thought my last response clarified the difference...

M_NOWAIT == I cannot wait for memory
M_WAITOK == I can wait for memory

You shouldn't ever wait for memory in critical code paths or while
holding simple locks.  I don't have time to search the documentation
for a more detailed explanation.

-Brian


