"Stefan Monnier <foo@acm.com>" <monnier+comp.arch/news/@flint.cs.yale.edu>
wrote in message news:5lhevaw76b.fsf@rum.cs.yale.edu...
> >>>>> "Maynard" == Maynard Handley <name99@mac.com> writes:
> > As that revenue stream flows into the company, it may be disbursed to
> > shareholders as dividends, or it may be used to grow the company
business,
> > or it may be used to invest in other companies, or it may be used for
> > empire building to grow the company into unrelated businesses. All of
> > these behaviors will have apologists; some have justification in tax
> > avoidance. Any halfway decent finance book will tell you about why they
> > occur and when they usually are or are not a good deal for shareholders.
>
> In the case where no dividend is payed, it seems that the only way
> in which something can be beneficial to shareholders is when it increases
> the value of those shares.  So shareholders buy such stock only because
> they hope that something will happen that will make other people believe
> that the share is worth more.  Sounds very much like poker to me.



You are assuming that there is no real intrinsic value underlying the stock.
This is not true.  the value is the ability to generate a stream of cash
flow (profits) in the future.  If the company doesn't distribute the cash to
the stockholders, it usually invests it by starting new projects or buying
existing ones, which are all designed to generate more cash.  the value of
that stream of cash, whether it is given back to you and you invest it or
whether the company invests it itself is the same (ignoring taxes and risk
preferences, etc.)  Hence, if the company generates more cash flow and
invests it themselves, the company becomes more valuable as it holds more
investments.  If it is more valuable, the stock price goes up.

 --
 -- Stephen Fuld





