Not at all. The money is (at least assuming management are neither
fraudulent nor incompetent) used to increase the value of the company,
either through purchases of real objects (buildings, computers, factory
eqipment) or by paying people to come up with IP owned by the company (for
example whatever you may think of MS and Windows, the money they have
spent internally on developing Windows has resulted in a product owned by
them that is worth a lot of money).

There is always the option for a company simply to disband and the assets
(after clearing all senior debt) to be returned to the stockholdes, and
that has happened. However generally, of course, the valuation to book
value ratio of a company is higher than 1 because it is believed by
stiockholders that the company (as a brand, as a collection of expertise,
as a group of employees who know each other and have some clue as to what
they are doing) is worth more than simply gutting it for assets.

Maynard


