"Kurt Shoens" <shoens@lenny.sfrn.dnai.com> wrote in message
news:9ki0rv$c88$1@bob.news.rcn.net...
> In article <9k5g1h$4fj$1@pyrite.mv.net>, Bill Todd <billtodd@foo.mv.com>
wrote:
> (paraphrasing, I hope fairly) that locks need to be fast but that locating
> pages in the buffer pool is already pretty expensive, so putting buffered
> pages in memory that's a system call away should be OK.

Not quite:  the statement was that the overall single-buffer-related
overhead of a database operation requiring access to a buffer likely
sufficiently eclipsed any additional overhead in mapping the buffer that
this added overhead was not a major problem.  Note that the total overhead
includes not only finding the buffer but finding whatever data is of
interest within the buffer, and then doing something with it.

>
> We'll have to agree to disagree on this point.  I can't see how to win
> with the buffer pool not directly addressable.  Having to put things out
> of addressability shakes up the rest of the system so much that you
> have to consider it a separate part of the memory hierarchy, like DB2's
> use of extended storage.

I did mention that frequently used items would indeed likely be cached at a
higher level (even with direct-mapped buffers, since targeting and then
finding stuff within a database page can be a bit expensive).  At a minimum,
I'd expect that the mapping window would act as at least a modest cache of
still-mapped recently-touched buffers.  So you could consider this an
additional layer of the memory hierarchy, though I suspect that the 'item
cache' (record cache?) is of more importance than the map-window 'cache'.

- bill





