This is the third in a series of "Tech Talks" by Terry Shannon. This Tech
Talk will be on The Truth about Tru64 UNIX

Other than that, Mrs. Lincoln, how did you like the play?

Compaq's June 25 IPF Consolidation announcement has some profound
implications for the Compaq user community, but it's not all bad news.
Seemingly overlooked in the IPF announcement were a number of Good Things
for the Tru64 UNIX constituency. Here's a quick look at what's new and
improved on the Tru64 front...

Any Port in a Storm?

Compaq's decision to abdicate the Alpha architecture upon the completion of
the final spin of the EV7 processor came as a surprise to just about
everyone, but Compaq's UNIX group is ideally suited to deal with the
architectural change of venue. In fact, SKC believes that Tru64 UNIX will be
the primary beneficiary of the architectural transition. Tru64 UNIX VP Kent
Ferson dedicated one third of his development team to the Itanium Processor
Family (IPF) port on the day the decision was announced, and when it comes
to architectural porting, Compaq's UNIX contingent definitely has a leg up
on its OpenVMS and NSK colleagues.

Longtime readers of Shannon Knows Compaq, not to mention longtime students
of Compaq UNIX history, will recall that Compaq successfully executed an
initial Tru64 to IA-64 port several years ago under the Bravo Initiative. A
joint venture between Compaq, Sequent, and other industry players,  Bravo
was envisioned as an IA64 UNIX implementation that leveraged the
capabilities of Tru64 UNIX together with NUMA capabilities and other
enhancements borrowed from Sequent's Dynix OS.

Sequent subsequently elected to embrace IBM's Project Monterey UNIX effort,
and the Oregon firm ultimately was acquired by IBM. Recognizing the limited
appeal of an IA64 UNIX port that was supported by just one mainstream
vendor, Compaq put the Bravo code up on the shelf even though the OS
successfully booted on a Merced emulator.

The Consolidation Cometh

Tru64 has made some very  significant advances in clustering, scalability,
and single system image support during the intervening era. In fact, Compaq
on July 16 announced a slew of Tru64 UNIX-related improvements, including
Oracle 9i RAC support, Aurema ARM enhancements, support for mixed-speed
CPUS, online CPU add and remove capabilities, and low-cost clustering. These
improvements underscore the eight consecutive quarters of market share
growth posted by Compaq's UNIX business.

What's next? It doesn't take an NDA to conclude that Compaq's future is IPF
and Tru64 UNIX, writ large. Absent such an NDA, plenty of conclusions can be
drawn. While porting Tru64 UNIX from Alpha to IPF will involve more than
taking the Bravo code down from the shelf and booting it on a ProLiant
Blazer system, Compaq can leverage a great deal of its existing development
effort to accelerate the availability of the new port.

I will be expanding on this and other subjects during the kickoff session on
Sunday night at the Compaq Enterprise Technical Symposium to be held in
Anaheim September 8-14. For more details please visit
http://www.CETS2001.com. I also routinely cover the Compaq cross currents in
my publication "Shannon Knows Compaq". For more details please visit
http://www.acersoft.com.

