PRESIDENT'S MESSAGE 
Welcome to the first edition of Encompass Points, the Encompass U.S. 
electronic newsletter. We plan on publishing this newsletter several 
times throughout the year to keep you informed of the plans, 
activities, and general information about your association and about 
Compaq. 

The first half of this year has certainly lived up to that prophetic 
saying, "may you live in interesting times." We have experienced the 
great bubble burst of the "dot-coms." We have seen our own high-tech 
industry take steep hits and have all come to know at least one or two 
associates (if not being one ourselves) that are no longer at their 
old positions. Closer to home, many within our own society are reeling 
from how to leverage, recover from, or otherwise simply fathom the 
significant announcement from Compaq that will see the transition from 
the Alpha technology to Intel's Itanium line. With all these changes 
and concerns affecting so many of us, it is not trite to say, "You are 
not alone." Encompass understands, and as your society, is working 
hard to pull together sources of information and forums for your 
discussions about these issues. 

Foremost among these is the upcoming Compaq Enterprise Technical 
Symposium (CETS) 2001 being held in Anaheim, September 8 through 14. 
While many other high-tech firms have canceled or deferred their 
technical conferences, Compaq, partnering with Encompass and others, 
continues to offer CETS. And never has this conference been more 
timely. Encompass is working closely with Compaq to provide a number 
of sessions and opportunities for you to really understand and probe 
Compaq's directions with Alpha and the implications for you and your 
business. More than discussing the future, CETS is the place to come 
to understand the "Here and Now" of the technologies, products and 
services you use today as well as tomorrow. Please see the article 
below on CETS for more information. 



Speaking of CETS, I also wanted to remind you that we will be holding 
the first annual Encompass member meeting at the conference. As 
members of the association, this is your meeting and your opportunity 
to meet other members of Board and me. Further details about this 
meeting will be e-mailed to you soon. 

I've already exceeded my line budget and there is still more to tell, 
but I'd better stop here. I look forward to seeing many of you at 
CETS. Have a safe journey. 

Joe Pollizzi 
President, Encompass, US 



CALL FOR BOARD OF DIRECTOR NOMINATIONS 
Encompass is accepting self-nominations for four Encompass board 
member positions for terms spanning 2002-2004. Interested candidates 
should e-mail contact information along with a statement of interest 
to information@encompassus.org by August 31. 

As part of our transition from our DECUS heritage to Encompass, three 
of the candidates will be elected to three-year terms, and the fourth 
to a one-year term. Newly elected board members will take office 
January 2002. Thereafter, Encompass will be electing three candidates 
each year. 

Members will vote on the new slate of candidates this fall. 



CETS PRE-CONFERENCE PROGRAM AND BOOTCAMPS 
Register by August 10 to save $100 on one-day Pre-conference Seminars 
or $200 on the two-day TechWorks Bootcamp. 

The Pre-Conference program continues as strong as ever and now 
includes the TechWorks Bootcamps. These are special pre-conference 
seminars specifically designed for the experienced computer 
professional who needs to learn a new technology. Even the bootcamps 
listed as "introductory" assume some level of technological savvy, 
such as the bootcamp for Windows NT or UNIX systems administrators who 
would like to learn OpenVMS systems management. Attendees are assumed 
to already know the "what" and "why" of managing a networked computer 
system from experience, so bootcamp trainers can jump right into the 
"how" of managing an OpenVMS system. At CETS2001, six excellent 
bootcamp topics are being presented by speaker teams of experienced 
trainers. 

Who should sign up for a TechWorks Bootcamp? Computer professionals 
who are looking to add a new technology to their site but who don't 
want to sit through "computer basics" before getting to the core of 
the new technology. Sign up for a seminar that gives you credit for 
your experience - sign up for a TechWorks Bootcamp! 

The registration information for the Bootcamps, as well as all other 
Pre-Conference Seminars, are found on the Seminar tab during the 
registration process (note: if you have already registered you can use 
the "Update Registration" feature on www.CETS2001.com to add these). 
We believe these intense Bootcamps will be the fast track to learning 
a "new to you" platform. For more information please download: 
http://www.cets2001.com/privatedocs/Seminars.pdf or 
http://www.cets2001.com/privatedocs/Seminars.txt 



