>|D1|D2|D3|D4|D5|D6 |		Unix sees this as /data1
>+---+---+----+----+----+--+

Regardless of the actual question, if all these disks are on the
same back-end SCSI bus (or just two busses), you're probably
limiting your performance.  The HSG80 has six back-end busses,
you should take advantage of that.

>
>The 6 disks are 18Gb each (bound volume of 108Gb), and I would like to
>upgrade, and double the capacity by using 36Gb disks (bound volume
>size of 216Gb).
>
>However, the time factor in backing up the data and restoring is
>somewhat daunting in this.
>
>I have been toying with the idea of daisy-chaining another SW cabinet
>(which we would probably rent), and placing the new disks in a shelf
>in that cabinet, and then set the logical volume up in the same manner
>as the "original" volume.

The HSG family controllers (and HSZ, HSD and HSJ) write metadata on
the disks they use.  Only "TRANSPORTABLE" disks don't get this meta-
data and those can't be used in storage sets.  While I'm doubtful
that you want to will work, the other Storageworks subsystem should
be the model and firmware as the one you have now.

>
>We would then do a disk to disk copy to the larger 36Gb drives "bound
>volume".

You need to be more precise here.  There are a couple ways of doing
this what will allow it work, but it amounts to the backup/restore
that you found so daunting earlier.  Across subsystems there's nothing
in the HSG you can use to make a physical backup.  The host doesn't
see the individual disks so you can't use that either.  A physical
backup of the logical unit will only copy the number of sectors in
the source unit and that won't change the size of file system.  That
pretty much leaves creating a target file system on the new larger
unit and using vdump, dump, tar or cpio to backup the source and
extract it to the target; your daunting problem without a tape in
the middle.

I also have to wonder at this point whether you have a backup of the
file system anyway.  RAID-5 is no replacement for a proper backup.
It can only protect against the loss of a single disk in the array.
It can't protect against a user accidently removing files.  It can't
protect against something that causes the loss every disk in the
cabinet.
  
>
>Then power the system down (including disks and controllers), remove
>the original 18Gb disks, and replace with the 36Gb disks, in the same
>order.

I don't know if the controller will handle the change in model or
capacity.  That probably does with the drive, but I've never tried
it.  To move an array of disks around you just need to repeat the
various ADD commands and skip the INIT command.  That much cleaning
is is probably supported.

>In theory, when we power the system back up, everything should "click"
>back in, except this time "/data1" would have doubled it's capacity.

On V5 there is a command that can extend a UFS if the underlying device
"grows".  But there isn't on V4.  And AdvFS still doesn't have this
support.

Of course if the underlying file system is AdvFS and you have the
utilities there is a much easier way to migrate the data:

1.  Create the new volume on the temporary subsystem.
2.  Label it.
3.  Add it to the existing domain with the addvol command.
4.  Remove the old volume with rmvol.
5.  Sit back and watch as AdvFS migrate the data from one volume
    to the other with the file system still available.
6.  Remove the old volume.

Shutdown and then move the disks.  Keeping the disks in the same
RAID order (physical location isn't relevant), add the disks,
RAID and unit.  Skip the INIT step.


