"Lars Poulsen" <lars@beagle-ears.com> wrote in message
news:3B679B0B.FB9AEDD6@beagle-ears.com...
> Maynard Handley wrote:
> > The bottom line is that the concept of company valuations is not
> > absurd and meaningless.
>
> The concept makes sense; it is just the actual valuations that don't.
> I have invested in companies whose business I knew intimately, and
> while the stock often fluctuated wildly, these fluctuations were
> totally unrelated (at least on a 1-to-6 month timescale) to any
> actual changes in the company's business conditions, as measured
> by order backlog, credit lines, employee morale and successes or
> failures in product development projects.




Sure, but they don't have to be.  For example, suppose a competitor
announces a new product that will be out in a year.  That might have no
immediate effect on your company but it might affect your long term
prospects and the stock price of your company would go down, even though
your company had no changes.  Or suppose that interest rates rise.  That
would encourage investors to put more money in bonds and less in stocks
which would also affect your company's stock price.  Or an election in say
Germany affects what people believe will be the future of the German
economy, which changes exchange rates and thus the relative atractivness of
your company's sotck.  Such changes are going on all the time which acounts
for a lot of the short term fluctuation.




--
    -  Stephen Fuld





