> > course, I've never figured out how you determine reasonable values - 10 is
> > better than 50, but how do you determine what is overvalued?
> 
> A good starting point is to actually go out and read an academic book
> about finance rather than posting ridiculous exaggerations on usenet.
> 
> A company represents a stream of revenue over time, no more or less than
> the stream of revenue from a loan or a mortgage. The main difference is
> that while the revenue stream from a loan or mortgage is very well to well
> characterized (subject only to uncertainty like refinancing), the revenue
> stream from a company is somewhat to extremely unpredictable.
> The value of a company at any time is essentially what the value of that
> revenue stream is believed to be by enough people who are willing to pay
> for that belief. (There may be many other people who do NOT believe that
> the revenue stream is worth that much, and they may or may not be correct,
> but their opinion doesn't matter.)
> As that revenue stream flows into the company, it may be disbursed to
> shareholders as dividends, or it may be used to grow the company business,
> or it may be used to invest in other companies, or it may be used for
> empire building to grow the company into unrelated businesses. All of
> these behaviors will have apologists; some have justification in tax
> avoidance. Any halfway decent finance book will tell you about why they
> occur and when they usually are or are not a good deal for shareholders.
> 
> The bottom line is that the concept of company valuations is not absurd
> and meaningless. There may of coure be situations where a large number of
> people agree that the valuations others place on a company are ridiculous,
> but reasonable people are allowed to disagree.
> If the stockmarket is gambling then, approached intelligently by people
> who understand how the system works, it is like being on the casino side
> of gambling. Of course, like anything else, jumping in clueless and
> assuming what you hear on CNNfn will make you rich is sheer stupidity, no
> different from deciding to take up flying by simply walking into the
> cockpit of a plane and deciding to play with the controls---sure the
> inbuilt stability of the plane and the safety features will protect you
> most of the time---but when something unexpected happens the results will
> not be pretty.
