I wouldn't be so quick to put Fred down if I were you Maynard.
A company might be considered to be a source of revenue over a period of
time (assuming that it can continue trading) but if I put out an
announcement tomorrow (or even just tell one or two contacts in the
press if such a thing is legal) that my company isn't going to meet its
revenue targets this year than the market loses confidence in my
company, sells its shares and my share price plummets.  I've seen it
happen.  It's not pretty but a multi-billion dollar company can lose
half of its perceived value overnight (as the company in question did)
and the fickle market takes time to build up confidence and share price
again - if indeed it ever does.

If one talks to certain authorities in this country they will be quite
helpful and suggest that you DON'T make a "profit" (I prefer the term
surplus since it has fewer connotations of champagne and wild parties -
thank you John Cleese and Ronnie Corbett for that concept) in the first
few years since it avoids any tax implications.

As for what is over valued Fred, it's like how many accountants does it
take to change a light bulb? "What kind of answer did you have in mind"
is the first answer, the other being "it depends".

Marks and Spencer in the UK used to trade (and may still) with a
liquidity or liquid ratio that in any other company would be suicidal. 
They worked on the concept that the goods would have turned over a
number of times before their suppliers' bills came due for payment so
they would already have the revenue that they had generated from the
sales in their bank.

