"Ginny E. Kim" wrote:

> > X  int     statesize = 5737;
> > X  static state_t **table = NULL;
> > X
> > X  int table_init()
> > X  {
> > X          MALLOC(table, state_t **, sizeof(state_t *) * statesize,
> > X  M_TEMP, M_NOWAIT);
> > X              ......................
> > X  }
> >
> > MALLOC is used when the size of the storage can be specified as either
> > a hard coded constant, a #define contant, or a sizeof(...) constant.
> >
> > MALLOC_VAR is used when the size of the storage is passed as a
> > variable.  'statesize' is a variable and thus you should be using
> > MALLOC_VAR
> >
> >                                         Bob Harris
>
> I used the size of the storage of MALLOC be specified as a hard coded constant.
> For instance,
>              #define STATESIZE 5737
>              statesize = STATESIZE
> I don't think 'statesize' is a variable.
> MALLOC() in Tru64 V4.0F doesn't have this problem.
> Doesn't the type or flag of malloc() have the problem?

Bob is absolutely right here, your code has a bug.  'statesize' is a global
variable, this means its value can be modified, for example with dbx.
For this reason, 'sizeof(state_t *) * statesize' cannot be calculated at
compile time, which is what's required if you're using MALLOC.

You have had a lot of questions here lately, most of which can be
answered by reading the documentation.  Please re-read some of it
again, you will probably notice a lot of things you didn't see the
first time now that you've had a chance to write some code.

-Brian


